\title{The Local Character of the Surreal Order Topology}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{August 30, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study the order topology on Conway's field $\No$ of surreal numbers and determine its local
structure completely. Our central result is that no point of $\No$ admits a small neighbourhood
basis: for every surreal $c$ and every set-indexed family $(B_i)_{i\in I}$ of neighbourhoods of
$c$, there exists a neighbourhood of $c$ containing no $B_i$. Consequently the character of every
point exceeds every cardinal, the neighbourhood filter $\Nb(c)$ is never countably generated, and
$\No$ is nowhere first countable and not metrizable. The proof has two ingredients: a
\emph{coinitiality failure} theorem, stating that for any set-indexed family $(r_i)$ of positive
surreals the Conway cut $\{0 \mid (r_i)\}$ is a positive surreal below all of them; and a
\emph{transfer} mechanism, namely that $\No$ with its order topology is a Hausdorff topological
additive group, so translations are homeomorphisms carrying the statement at $0$ to every point.
We give a second, independent proof via \emph{sequential discreteness}: a sequence in $\No$
converges if and only if it is eventually constant, while $\No$ is not discrete. We situate these
results within a complete description of the local topology: the archimedean monads form a clopen
neighbourhood basis at every point, making $\No$ zero-dimensional and totally separated (hence
admitting no nonconstant continuous map from a connected space); and no neighbourhood of any point
is compact, since the upper half of a monad has no least upper bound.
\end{abstract}

